KG42.register("source_edge_archive-e158186715201dd5695eb3a6",{"id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R007::E::v141-c-R007-E001","paper":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets","rule_id":"v141-c-R007","rule_ref":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R007","record":{"edge_id":"v141-c-R007-E001","source":"pricing.local","target":"settlement.payment","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R007-C001"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E05","v141-b-g01-E08","v141-b-g01-E10"]},"evidence_class":"S","provenance_gate":"direct_C_edge_with_constraint_and_source_excerpt","scientific_acceptance":false,"accepted_constraint_ids":["v141-c-R007-C001"],"held_constraint_ids":[],"semantic_review_status":"supported","source":"COMP-535650ecb2e046e9","target":"COMP-739917e3869e45a6","source_occurrence_id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R007::V::pricing.local","target_occurrence_id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-c-R007::V::settlement.payment","source_unit_id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-a-M01::pricing.local","target_unit_id":"22-decentralized_flexibility_markets::v141-a-M01::settlement.payment","omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"settlement","context_text":"Agents labelled prosumers 46-55 in the reported simulation case studies. | Bilateral flexibility contracts within the distribution-network market; prices are specific to individual contracts and metering intervals. | Prosumers 1-30 in Case Study B. | Reported simulation case-study outcomes in the flexibility market.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E05","v141-b-g01-E08","v141-b-g01-E10","v141-b-g03-E11"]},"active":true,"source_signature":"f2ec5232accdfc31060cd6b7038b89cb84d3d813bb2995e6636aab9aed8b49ba","relation_scope":"original_occurrence_pair_only","internal_to_parent":false,"source_science":{"mechanism_id":"v141-a-M01","source_definition":"Iterative price-signal coordination produces mutually agreed prices for local flexibility contracts. Scope: Bilateral flexibility contracts within the distribution-network market. Condition: At negotiation completion, when no price changes occur during an iteration. Each discrete unit of flexibility may be individually priced. Scope: Distribution-network flexibility contracts specifying equal energy quantities of demand reduction and discretizing flexibility in energy and time. Each trade is associated with at most one contract and therefore has a unique price. Scope: Individual flexibility trades in the distribution-network market. Condition: The selected contract set is a feasible market outcome.","target_definition":"The agreed local contract price specifies the amount the seller receives from the buyer for the contracted demand reduction. Scope: Bilateral flexibility contracts within the distribution-network market; prices are specific to individual contracts and metering intervals. Condition: For contracts agreed by their buyer and seller. Payments made by the DSO match the combined revenue accumulated by prosumers and aggregators in the reported outcomes. Scope: Reported simulation case-study outcomes in the flexibility market. Prosumers 46-55 receive no flexibility-market revenue because they do not participate. Scope: Agents labelled prosumers 46-55 in the reported simulation case studies. Condition: These agents do not participate in the flexibility market. Prosumers 1-30 must be compensated for their higher battery degradation costs and consequently receive significantly more revenue in Case Study B. Scope: Prosumers 1-30 in Case Study B. Condition: Their battery degradation costs are higher in Case Study B.","constraints":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R007-C001","kind":"mathematical","role":"core","statement":"The agreed local contract price specifies the amount the seller receives from the buyer for the contracted demand reduction. Scope: Bilateral flexibility contracts within the distribution-network market; prices are specific to individual contracts and metering intervals. Condition: For contracts agreed by their buyer and seller.","expression":"\\forall \\omega\\in\\Omega:\\ \\bigl[(\\omega,t_\\omega,p_\\omega)\\text{ is an agreed contract with buyer }b(\\omega)\\text{ and seller }s(\\omega)\\bigr]\\Rightarrow \\text{the seller }s(\\omega)\\text{ receives }p_\\omega\\text{ from the buyer }b(\\omega).","formalization_origin":"source","template_id":null,"members":["pricing.local","settlement.payment"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":["param_contract_price"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E05","v141-b-g01-E08","v141-b-g01-E10","v141-b-g01-E13"]}],"evidence":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E05","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p03-c1r3__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Decentralized Flexibility Market Design","anchor_text":"[44]. Each prosumer has a retail supplier, which meters their th individual net energy use (e.g. dur","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"The proposed flexibility market works alongside existing retail electricity market arrangements in a distribution network. It is assumed that the distribution network has a number of prosumers–consumers with flexible energy resources, and equipment for automated resource scheduling based on user settings, such as a building or home energy management system [44]. Each prosumer has a retail supplier, which meters their individual net energy use (e.g. during each 30 minute interval of the day). For each interval, each prosumer is charged for their net energy use, at prices which may be time-of-use dependent, and paid for their net exported energy [45]."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E08","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p03-c2r2__v141-a-M01-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Decentralized Flexibility Market Design; Fig. 1","anchor_text":"mer xis- ce- ies ar- Fig. 1. A trading network for distribution system ﬂexibility between a DSO, , i","equation_id":null,"figure_id":"Fig. 1","table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"The flexibility market is made up of a network of potential flexibility contracts, with multiple contracts connecting each pair of agents that are able to trade with one another. Fig. 1 shows a high-level diagram of a trading network for distribution system flexibility. Each flexibility contract specifies a price the seller will receive from the buyer for reducing demand during a particular retail market metering interval."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E10","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p03-c2r3__v141-a-M01-bidding","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":"II. Decentralized Flexibility Market Design","anchor_text":"eir their willingness to accept a contract specifying a price for a unit of of demand reduction duri","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"The agents reach mutual agreement on a set of agreed contracts and contract prices through an iterative price-negotiation mechanism. At each step of the market, agents select their favorite bundle of available upstream and downstream contracts, and over-demanded contracts have their prices increased. The process continues until no price changes occur during an iteration."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E13","location":{"location_id":"src-12c011a9b5-p04-c1r1__v141-a-M01-participation","page":4,"printed_page":null,"section":"III. Agent Preferences","anchor_text":"MORSTYN et al.: DESIGNING DECENTRALIZED MARKETS FOR DISTRIBUTION SYSTE large number of consumers and","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-04.png","occurrence_role":"unknown"},"source_text":"Let A be the set of agents in the trading network. In the flexibility market, the agents trade flexibility contracts for a set of time intervals \\(\\mathcal{T} = \\{1, \\ldots, T\\}\\). Each flexibility contract specifies a price for a flexibility trade, specifying an agreed reduction in demand during a particular time interval. Note that all trades specify the same energy quantity of demand reduction. Let \\(X\\) be the set of contracts between the agents. A contract \\(x \\in X\\) can be described by a tuple \\((\\omega, t_\\omega, p_\\omega)\\), where \\(\\omega \\in \\Omega\\) is a unique trade ID, \\(t_\\omega\\) is the time interval of the underlying trade and \\(p_\\omega\\) is the price specified by the contract for the trade. The trades underlying a set of contracts \\(X\\) are given by \\(\\tau(X) = \\{\\omega \\in \\Omega | (\\omega, t_\\omega, p_\\omega) \\in X\\}\\)."}],"omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"settlement","context_text":"Agents labelled prosumers 46-55 in the reported simulation case studies. | Bilateral flexibility contracts within the distribution-network market; prices are specific to individual contracts and metering intervals. | Prosumers 1-30 in Case Study B. | Reported simulation case-study outcomes in the flexibility market.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E05","v141-b-g01-E08","v141-b-g01-E10","v141-b-g03-E11"]},"source_record":{"edge_id":"v141-c-R007-E001","source":"pricing.local","target":"settlement.payment","type":"links","constraint_ids":["v141-c-R007-C001"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E05","v141-b-g01-E08","v141-b-g01-E10"]},"paper_scope_note":"The temporarily selected excess-demand feedback differs from the final contract configuration; the final price-to-payment relation is limited to the same concluded contract and metering interval."}});
